CoolerControl for Sparky Stereo OS
==================================

Where things are
  service        coolercontrold.service (enabled when the package is installed; systemctl disable --now coolercontrold keeps it off across upgrades)
  interface      http://localhost:11987 (served by the service), and the window /usr/bin/coolercontrol (package coolercontrol)
  settings       /etc/coolercontrol (config.toml, written by the service), /var/lib/coolercontrol; the package never ships, writes or replaces them. Removing the package keeps them; purging it removes them (where no copy of upstream's makefile shares the folders)

If a copy made by upstream's own makefile is on the computer
  `make install` puts coolercontrold in /usr/local/bin (or /usr/bin) and its unit in /usr/local/lib/systemd/system or /etc/systemd/system, which systemd reads before /usr/lib/systemd/system, and it shares /etc/coolercontrol with this package. Installing this package then changes nothing of that copy: its service is not enabled, started, restarted or stopped, and no setting is touched. Nothing here removes your copy for you.

What the package does not do
  Upstream's package rewrites the computer's apt sources to the project's own repository and installs its archive keyring when it is installed. This package does not: the sources of the package manager are the person's.

Who owns a fan or a pump
  A device two programs could open has one owner on this edition (sparky-stereo-hardware): Corsair's hubs and coolers stay OpenLinkHub's, an AMD card's fan is LACT's while LACT is installed, the rest is CoolerControl's. In CoolerControl a person can still disable any device or channel in the interface.
